WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Film Courage: We had a question come in from&nbsp;
our viewer Ernesto Caro and Ernesto writes “My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.960
biggest curiosity is the pacing of the story.&nbsp;
I'm a screenwriter and I love comics but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:17.644
couldn't even imagine how to pace a comic book&nbsp;
in regard to dialogue versus action per page?”

00:00:17.644 --> 00:00:20.160
Stephen L. Stern, Comic Book Author/President of&nbsp;
Storyboard Graphic Novels: Okay, now in order for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:28.720
me to answer this question I need to talk a little&nbsp;
bit about this company I established 10 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:40.520
actually in 2011 so back in 2011 it was quite&nbsp;
clear to me that there was a real nexus between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.280
graphic novels and comics. On the one hand and&nbsp;
film and animation on the other by which I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:56.760
to say more and more movies were being based&nbsp;
on comic books and graphic novels for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:05.400
most people are familiar with the movie Road&nbsp;
to Perdition what most people don't know is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:15.880
that started its life as a graphic novel it was&nbsp;
written by well-known mystery novelist Max Allen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:26.800
Collins and it was taken to Hollywood by a fellow&nbsp;
named Ken F. Levin who is also the publisher and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:38.720
founder of 1First Comics that published is Zen&nbsp;
[Zen Intergalactic Ninja] okay and so I knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:48.080
there was a real connection between movies and&nbsp;
comic books and so I came up with this concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:58.840
of a company that is called Storyboard Graphic&nbsp;
Novels and it essentially says to screenwriters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:06.080
but also producers directors and actors give us&nbsp;
your screenplay and we'll turn it into a graphic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:19.240
novel or a comic book series and this brings up&nbsp;
the answer to your viewers question what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:25.920
is this okay after we sign a contract and we&nbsp;
were on board with a screenwriter who say has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:37.840
a 100 page screenplay okay what happens is I&nbsp;
read the screenplay and I start thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:48.680
an appropriate artist for the genre of the book is&nbsp;
superhero is it a romcom is it horror is it sci-fi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:59.760
right at the same time I'm doing that I personally&nbsp;
write the adaptation the graphic novel adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:08.840
many of my fellow writers in the industry have&nbsp;
asked me hey Steve could I write some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:18.040
adaptations for you thus far I've personally&nbsp;
written every adaptation which is over 80 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:28.040
them since 2011 it's probably closer to 100 now&nbsp;
the reason I do that is I always invoke this quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:36.760
from Liam in the movie Taken. Do you remember&nbsp;
when he said I have a particular set of skills?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:44.200
Well I do, I have a particular set of skills.&nbsp;
I know how to take a movie script and convert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:55.360
it into a graphic novel script and some of the&nbsp;
things that relate to that are how there's a page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:03.960
of a screenplay is equivalent to a minute on the&nbsp;
screen well a page of a screenplay is equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:10.520
to a page and a third in a graphic novel. In&nbsp;
other words it takes more than a one page of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:16.680
a screenplay to get it into a graphic novel.&nbsp;
It's going to be like a page in a third and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:24.960
this algorithm if you want to call it has proved&nbsp;
correct for like dozens and dozens of books. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:34.800
came up with that algorithm and it works right but&nbsp;
in terms of the facing and what your viewer asked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:46.160
you have to know how to take a screenplay which&nbsp;
is written for the fluid medium of film and turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:54.320
it into a script for the static medium of a&nbsp;
comic book or graphic novel because a book is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:01.600
static. It's panel after panel after panel after&nbsp;
panel. It's called sequential art okay and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:09.680
is something because I've done it so frequently&nbsp;
I know how to do it and part and parcel of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:20.000
is also knowing things like how to keep a page&nbsp;
cinematic by not only not overburdening it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:30.640
too many panels but just as important is how&nbsp;
many words you can put on a page how many word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:39.280
balloons maximum can go in a panel so these&nbsp;
are all like formulas if you will to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:51.720
that the story moves along at the right pace is&nbsp;
still cinematic looking and isn't overburdened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.720 --> 00:06:02.240
by text which is word balloons and captions&nbsp;
so again to explain how to do this would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:10.960
tantamount to explaining how to write a movie&nbsp;
script right and that's something that we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:18.280
have enough time to discuss here oh sure but&nbsp;
I'm curious as a rule how many word balloons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:26.560
are considered sort of the standard acceptable&nbsp;
per panel two or three but it all depends how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:36.200
many words are in each balloon when you get into&nbsp;
like a soliloquy or a monologue you may have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:45.880
divide up what the character is saying into like&nbsp;
four panels because there's also like a maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:51.440
number of words you can put in a balloon which&nbsp;
is like 12 if you really must know right these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:07:04.480
are all formulas that I've worked out right and&nbsp;
yeah so pacing characters cinematic nature how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:13.320
much dialogue to put in it's it's an art and&nbsp;
a science that's great information thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

